WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.680
When we're dealing
with decimal numbers,

00:01.680 --> 00:04.400
rounding is an important tool
that we need to know how to use.

00:04.400 --> 00:06.160
And in order to see why,

00:06.160 --> 00:08.560
let's take a look at
an extreme example.

00:08.560 --> 00:10.880
So let's say we've been given
this decimal number here.

00:10.880 --> 00:13.280
It's one hundred two
point three seven four,

00:13.280 --> 00:14.955
then a bunch of zeros,

00:14.955 --> 00:18.315
and then this little one hanging
out all the way at the end.

00:18.315 --> 00:22.635
And what we should notice is
that this one only makes a

00:22.635 --> 00:26.635
tiny, tiny, tiny, tiny, tiny
difference to the value of this

00:26.635 --> 00:28.395
overall number as a whole.

00:28.395 --> 00:31.890
So writing out all these extra
zeros just for the sake of

00:31.890 --> 00:35.410
including this one doesn't
make that much sense.

00:35.410 --> 00:39.090
It'd be a lot more practical
instead to write this number as

00:39.090 --> 00:42.530
just approximately one
hundred two point three

00:42.530 --> 00:46.015
seven four and drop all these
extra zeros and this tiny

00:46.015 --> 00:50.335
little one that really has very little
effect on the value of this number.

00:50.335 --> 00:52.975
This number here, one zero
two point three seven four,

00:52.975 --> 00:55.855
is so close to the actual
number that it doesn't even

00:55.855 --> 00:57.100
really make a difference.

00:57.100 --> 00:58.380
So when we're rounding,

00:58.380 --> 01:01.820
what we're doing is
approximating a decimal number

01:01.820 --> 01:05.100
with fewer than the number of
digits in the actual value.

01:05.100 --> 01:07.820
And the reason we do that is
because it saves us a lot of

01:07.820 --> 01:12.355
time and lets us express really long
numbers as shorter simpler ones.

01:12.355 --> 01:16.755
Now when we round, we always
round to a certain decimal place.

01:16.755 --> 01:18.755
So we've already looked
at decimal places.

01:18.755 --> 01:20.035
We know what they are.

01:20.035 --> 01:23.075
So here, if we're asked
to round to, for example,

01:23.075 --> 01:24.320
the hundredths place,

01:24.320 --> 01:27.280
what that means is that we want
the last digit in the number to

01:27.280 --> 01:28.960
be the digit in the
hundredths place.

01:28.960 --> 01:31.280
So if we were rounding
this decimal number to the

01:31.280 --> 01:34.560
hundredths place, we would
identify the hundredths place here,

01:34.560 --> 01:37.840
and we would want the last
digit in the rounded number to

01:37.840 --> 01:39.280
be the digit in that spot.

01:39.280 --> 01:42.615
We wouldn't wanna see any other
digits after the hundreds place.

01:42.615 --> 01:46.535
So when we rounded this number to one
hundred two point three seven four,

01:46.535 --> 01:49.095
we were rounding to the
thousands place because that's

01:49.095 --> 01:50.535
where the four is sitting,

01:50.535 --> 01:53.980
and that's the last digit
that appears in the rounded number.

01:53.980 --> 01:56.700
Now our rule for rounding
is always the same.

01:56.700 --> 01:58.140
It's this rule here.

01:58.140 --> 02:01.100
So if the next digit
is less than five,

02:01.100 --> 02:03.260
we round the
previous digit down.

02:03.260 --> 02:06.700
If it's five or greater, we
round the previous digit up.

02:06.700 --> 02:10.435
In other words, we can just
remember this, less than five,

02:10.435 --> 02:11.875
we round down.

02:11.875 --> 02:14.595
Five or greater, we round up.

02:14.595 --> 02:17.155
So here's what that
means in practicality.

02:17.155 --> 02:19.635
Let's say we're actually this
time starting with this number

02:19.635 --> 02:21.795
here, one hundred two
point three seven four,

02:21.795 --> 02:25.800
and we've been asked to round to the
hundreds place like we said before.

02:25.800 --> 02:28.600
That's the place where we
currently see the seven.

02:28.600 --> 02:31.320
So if we're rounding
to the hundreds place,

02:31.320 --> 02:32.840
we look at the next digit,

02:32.840 --> 02:34.520
the digit that follows
the hundreds place,

02:34.520 --> 02:37.835
which in this case is the digit
in the thousands place, the four.

02:37.835 --> 02:40.235
Now we look at that four
and we ask ourselves,

02:40.235 --> 02:43.675
is that number less than five,
or is it five or greater?

02:43.675 --> 02:45.675
Is it equal to five
or greater than five?

02:45.675 --> 02:47.755
Well, four is less than five,

02:47.755 --> 02:49.835
which means that
we're rounding down.

02:49.835 --> 02:53.060
Now here's what it means
to round down or round up.

02:53.060 --> 02:55.760
When we're rounding down,

02:56.100 --> 02:59.280
that means that we're
leaving the digit

03:00.100 --> 03:01.200
unchanged.

03:01.380 --> 03:06.865
When we're rounding up, we
increase the digit by one unit

03:06.865 --> 03:08.705
or we add one to the digit.

03:08.705 --> 03:11.505
So because here four
is less than five,

03:11.505 --> 03:12.865
that means we're rounding down.

03:12.865 --> 03:15.745
And to round down means
to leave the digit unchanged.

03:15.745 --> 03:20.710
So we leave the seven unchanged
and the rounded number when we

03:20.710 --> 03:24.070
round one zero two point three
seven four to the hundredths place,

03:24.070 --> 03:27.110
we leave the seven unchanged
because we were rounding down

03:27.110 --> 03:31.270
and the rounded number is one
zero two point three seven.

03:31.270 --> 03:32.230
If on the other hand,

03:32.230 --> 03:37.085
we had started with one zero
two point three seven eight

03:37.085 --> 03:40.205
and we were again asked to
round to the hundreds place,

03:40.205 --> 03:42.525
we would look at the digit that
follows the hundreds place.

03:42.525 --> 03:43.965
In this case, it's an eight.

03:43.965 --> 03:46.765
We would see that eight
is five or greater.

03:46.765 --> 03:47.885
And because it's
five or greater,

03:47.885 --> 03:49.540
that means we have to round up,

03:49.540 --> 03:52.900
which means we have to
increase the seven by one.

03:52.900 --> 03:56.500
And therefore, this
number rounds to one

03:56.500 --> 04:00.420
hundred two point three
eight when we're rounding

04:00.420 --> 04:01.780
to the hundreds place.

04:01.780 --> 04:03.895
So So let's go ahead and look
at a couple more examples.

04:03.895 --> 04:07.575
Let's say that we've been given the
number three point one four one five nine,

04:07.575 --> 04:10.455
and we've been asked to round
it to the nearest hundredth.

04:10.455 --> 04:14.150
So the first thing we do is identify
the digit in the hundredth place.

04:14.150 --> 04:16.630
In this case, that's
this four right here.

04:16.630 --> 04:18.310
Now that we've
identified the digit,

04:18.310 --> 04:19.910
we look at the next digit.

04:19.910 --> 04:22.790
In this case, it's this
one that follows the four.

04:22.790 --> 04:25.510
We ask ourselves, is
one less than five,

04:25.510 --> 04:27.190
or is one five or greater?

04:27.190 --> 04:29.125
Well, one is less than five,

04:29.125 --> 04:30.805
which means we're rounding down,

04:30.805 --> 04:33.685
which means we leave
the four unchanged.

04:33.685 --> 04:38.565
So this number gets
rounded to three point one

04:38.565 --> 04:41.045
four when we're rounding
to the hundredths place.

04:41.045 --> 04:44.660
If we were rounding instead,
let's say to this place here,

04:44.660 --> 04:47.380
where the five is, we would
look at the next digit,

04:47.380 --> 04:48.980
we would see that it's nine.

04:48.980 --> 04:52.180
Nine is five or greater,
which means we round up,

04:52.180 --> 04:54.820
which means that we
change the five to a six,

04:54.820 --> 04:59.220
and that number would round
to three point one four

04:59.220 --> 05:00.715
one six.

05:00.715 --> 05:02.635
Let's do one more example.

05:02.635 --> 05:05.755
This time, we have one hundred
thirty thousand eight hundred seventy

05:05.755 --> 05:06.955
four point six two,

05:06.955 --> 05:09.675
and we've been asked to
round to the nearest hundred.

05:09.675 --> 05:12.475
Not the hundredths place,
but the hundredths place,

05:12.475 --> 05:14.420
which is this place right here.

05:14.420 --> 05:17.460
The four is in the units place,
the seven is in the tens place,

05:17.460 --> 05:19.300
the eight is in
the hundreds place.

05:19.300 --> 05:23.140
So, again, we look at the next
digit that follows the eight,

05:23.140 --> 05:25.380
that's this seven here
in the tens place,

05:25.380 --> 05:26.740
and we ask ourselves,

05:26.740 --> 05:29.675
is seven less than five
or five or greater?

05:29.675 --> 05:32.875
It's obviously five or greater,
which means we're rounding up,

05:32.875 --> 05:35.435
which means we add
one to the eight,

05:35.435 --> 05:39.655
which means we round to one hundred
thirty thousand nine hundred.

05:39.995 --> 05:43.940
Notice how when we round to a
digit that sits to the right of

05:43.940 --> 05:44.740
the decimal point,

05:44.740 --> 05:47.220
like we did here when we
rounded to the hundredths place

05:47.220 --> 05:49.460
or we rounded to the
ten thousandths place,

05:49.460 --> 05:52.900
we made that digit the
last digit in the number.

05:52.900 --> 05:54.260
Here, on the other hand,

05:54.260 --> 05:57.695
when we rounded to a digit that
sat to the left of the decimal

05:57.695 --> 06:00.175
point, here the eight sat to
the left of the decimal point,

06:00.175 --> 06:01.295
we rounded to that number.

06:01.295 --> 06:04.095
So we looked at whether or not
we needed to change the value

06:04.095 --> 06:06.335
of the eight based on
the value of the seven,

06:06.335 --> 06:10.030
but then we kept zeros all
the way to the units place,

06:10.030 --> 06:11.710
and that's always what we'll do.

06:11.710 --> 06:14.190
You wouldn't round one hundred
thirty thousand eight hundred

06:14.190 --> 06:17.550
and seventy four to just one
three zero nine or one thousand

06:17.550 --> 06:18.670
three hundred nine.

06:18.670 --> 06:18.910
No.

06:18.910 --> 06:20.430
We're rounding to
the nearest hundred,

06:20.430 --> 06:22.955
so one hundred thirty thousand
eight hundred and seventy four.

06:22.955 --> 06:26.475
The closest hundred to that is one
hundred thirty thousand nine hundred.

06:26.475 --> 06:29.755
So we fill in the rest of the
digits that are to the left of

06:29.755 --> 06:31.995
the decimal point with zeros.

06:31.995 --> 06:33.915
And that is where we stop.

06:33.915 --> 06:36.930
We don't keep any digits to
the right of the decimal point.

06:36.930 --> 06:39.970
And then there's one last thing
we wanna say about rounding and

06:39.970 --> 06:42.370
we can illustrate it by
changing this number here.

06:42.370 --> 06:45.170
So instead of this one hundred
thirty thousand eight seventy

06:45.170 --> 06:48.690
four, let's say we had one
hundred thirty thousand nine

06:48.690 --> 06:52.125
hundred seventy four instead of
eight hundred and seventy four

06:52.125 --> 06:53.485
point six two.

06:53.485 --> 06:57.405
And again, we were asked to round
this number to the nearest hundred.

06:57.405 --> 06:59.245
So same hundreds place,

06:59.245 --> 07:01.965
we would again look at this
seven that's sitting just to

07:01.965 --> 07:03.090
the right of the nine.

07:03.090 --> 07:05.250
We would see that seven
is five or greater,

07:05.250 --> 07:08.610
and so we would need to round
up the nine by increasing that

07:08.610 --> 07:09.730
digit by one.

07:09.730 --> 07:12.130
But we can't round
a nine to a ten.

07:12.130 --> 07:14.130
That would be replacing
one digit with two,

07:14.130 --> 07:15.330
which doesn't work.

07:15.330 --> 07:18.745
So what we do in the special
case where we're rounding up a

07:18.745 --> 07:23.065
nine is we change that nine to
a zero and increase the next

07:23.065 --> 07:24.425
digit by one.

07:24.425 --> 07:28.105
So this nine here is
gonna become a zero,

07:28.105 --> 07:31.385
and we're going to increase
the digit to the left of it,

07:31.385 --> 07:33.530
this zero right here, by one.

07:33.530 --> 07:35.370
So instead of one
hundred thirty thousand,

07:35.370 --> 07:38.010
this is gonna become one
hundred thirty one thousand.

07:38.010 --> 07:42.250
So rounding this up, we get
one hundred thirty one thousand

07:42.250 --> 07:45.335
and then again, the
nine becomes a zero.

07:45.335 --> 07:47.895
All the digits to the right
of the nine up to the decimal

07:47.895 --> 07:50.535
place get filled in with
zeros just like before,

07:50.535 --> 07:53.415
and we don't include any digits
to the right of the decimal

07:53.415 --> 07:56.375
point, and we end up with one
hundred thirty one thousand.

07:56.375 --> 07:58.370
So those are our
rules for rounding.

07:58.370 --> 08:01.170
The biggest thing here is to
remember that if that next

08:01.170 --> 08:03.330
digit is less than
five, we round down.

08:03.330 --> 08:05.170
If it's five or
greater, we round up.

08:05.170 --> 08:07.490
And rounding down means
we leave the digit we're

08:07.490 --> 08:08.930
interested in unchanged.

08:08.930 --> 08:12.750
Rounding up means we
increase it by one.
